<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Chapter 2</a> &gt; Level 25</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 25 Walkthrough: Restroom</h1>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <p>Level 25 drops you in a corridor between two restrooms and asks two real questions: can you make every side of the floor-drain lid add up to <b>61</b>, and can you turn four paper grids into a click pattern on a 4x4 panel? Everything else — and there is a lot of everything else — is a tool hunt across both rooms, and the order you do it in is the difference between a nine-minute level and a very long one.</p>

    <div class="verify-note">Rebuilt from two independent recordings of the current build: a 9:16 playthrough of Level 25, extracted at one frame per second and re-read at four frames per second around the drain, the papers and the exit, and a 6:34 second run used as a cross-check. The first video's clock reads 9:01 at the results screen and the file runs 9:16, so the two are close enough that the timestamps below point at the video rather than at the in-game clock. Every number printed here was read at least twice: the <b>61</b> in the centre of the drain, the four paper grids, the <b>+50</b> and the <b>9:01</b>. Item names are the game's own labels, pulled off the raw frames with optical character recognition. Where the footage cannot settle something, the page says so at the bottom instead of filling the gap in.</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={2} current={25} variant="jump" label="Chapter 2 · No Honor Among Thieves" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>No keypad, no letter code: Level 25 is a drain lid that has to total <b>61</b> and a 4x4 panel that has to be clicked <b>29</b> times in a fixed pattern. Finish worth <b>+50 coins</b>.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Tap the <b>potted plant</b> in the corridor → <b>Key</b>. Use it on the door with the <b>male symbol</b>.</li>
        <li>In the men's room take the <b>Gloves</b> off the counter, the <b>Plunger</b> off the cleaning cart, and the <b>Hook</b> that a red wall button pays out in the end stall.</li>
        <li>Solve the <b>floor drain</b>: every side of the circular lid must add up to <b>61</b>, with two-digit tiles counted as separate digits. Then <b>Hook</b> the key out of the open drain.</li>
        <li>Clear the <b>sink</b> for a <b>Crumpled piece of paper</b> → <b>Dismantle</b> → <b>Piece of paper</b> + <b>Key</b>. Clean the mirrors with soap and a towel; a good <b>Light bulb</b> over the right-hand mirror lights it and pays out another paper.</li>
        <li>Take the <b>Utensil</b> from the women's room back to the corridor plant to dig out a second key, and break the <b>PIPES</b> poster for a <b>Rolled piece of paper</b>.</li>
        <li>Feed the 4x4 panel beside the exit doorway: <b>3 0 2 1 / 2 4 0 0 / 3 1 3 0 / 2 2 2 4</b> clicks per cell.</li>
        <li>The panel completes, the camera cuts to the corridor, and the level ends on <b>+50 coins</b> and <b>Time: 9:01</b>.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
            <div class="yt-facade" data-yt="uopc_Ls173Q" data-title="Rooms &amp;amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 25 Restroom walkthrough"
           role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Play video: Rooms &amp;amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 25 Restroom walkthrough">
        <img src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/uopc_Ls173Q/maxresdefault.jpg"
             alt="Rooms &amp;amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 25 Restroom walkthrough" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1280" height="720"
             onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src='https://i.ytimg.com/vi/uopc_Ls173Q/hqdefault.jpg'">
      </div>
    </div>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l25-hero-hallway.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 2 Level 25: the corridor outside the restrooms, with a locked door on the left, the men's door in the middle, the women's door on the right and a tall potted plant between them" width="1200" height="594" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Level 25 is called <b>Restrooms</b> on the game's own title card — plural, and that is the whole design of the room. You start in a short corridor with a striped floor and three doors: a plain door on the left carrying a small lock panel, the <b>men's</b> door in the middle, and the <b>women's</b> door on the right, with a tall potted plant standing between the two labelled ones. Behind those two doors are two separate restrooms, and the level is spread across both of them plus the corridor that joins them.</p>
    <p>The men's room is the bigger of the two: a row of green stalls against the left wall, three urinals on the back wall under a run of exposed pipes, a long green counter with three basins and a wide mirror on the right, a cleaning cart parked in the middle of the floor, and the door back to the corridor in the far corner with a male figure on it. The women's room is a different palette — yellow stall doors, four basins on a purple counter, no urinals at all — and it is where the level hides its smaller pick-ups: a pink pouch, a pedal bin, a caged wall lamp and a hand dryer with a hole where its button should be.</p>
    <p>What makes the room feel longer than nine minutes is that almost nothing is where the thing that opens it is. The key to the men's door is in a plant in the corridor. The key that opens the drain is inside the drain, on a string, and you cannot reach it until you have solved the lid. The paper that tells you the exit pattern is in the wall behind a poster, in a compartment that only opens once the poster is broken. The level is a chain of small tools, each one spent on a different fixture, and the two puzzles that actually stop people — the drain arithmetic and the 4x4 click panel — are at opposite ends of it.</p>
    <p>It is also, for the record, one of the few Chapter 2 rooms with no numeric code at all. There is no keypad, no safe dial, no four-digit lock. Every number in the level is either a target sum or a count of clicks, which is why a guide that just hands you "the code" is no help here.</p>

    <h2>The room, fixture by fixture<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Before the steps, here is the map we wish we had on the first run. Everything in the level is one of these objects, and each one belongs to exactly one step.</p>
    <table class="item-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Object</th><th>Where</th><th>What it is for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Potted plant</td><td>Corridor, between the two restroom doors</td><td>Two separate pay-outs. First tap gives the <b>Key</b> for the men's door; a later tap with the <b>Utensil</b> digs out a second key.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Locked door</td><td>Corridor, left wall</td><td>A small panel rather than a handle. Stays shut for the whole level.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mosaic panel</td><td>Corridor, beside the exit doorway</td><td>The 4x4 click grid. The last lock in the level.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cleaning cart</td><td>Men's room, centre of the floor</td><td>Three tools over the run: <b>Plunger</b>, <b>Spray bottle</b>, <b>Towel</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Counter and sinks</td><td>Men's room, right wall</td><td><b>Gloves</b> on the counter; standing water in one basin; the <b>Soap</b> dispenser is on the tiled wall beside them.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Floor drain</td><td>Men's room, floor by the cart</td><td>The arithmetic ring. Centre reads <b>61</b>; solving it lifts the square lid.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>End stall</td><td>Men's room, left wall</td><td>A red button on the tiled wall pays out the <b>Hook</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>PIPES poster</td><td>Men's room, blue tiled wall over the sinks</td><td>Covers a recessed compartment. Bears the number <b>23919</b> in a red oval, which is signage, not a code.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wall lamp and mirrors</td><td>Women's room, over the basins</td><td>A caged lamp holding a <b>Light bulb</b>, and mirrors that stay dark until one is lit.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pouch and pedal bin</td><td>Women's room, counter and floor</td><td><b>Nail file</b> from the pouch, <b>Box of food</b> from the bin, <b>Utensil</b> from dismantling the box.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hand dryer</td><td>Women's room, wall by the door</td><td>A socket where a <b>Machine button</b> goes. Fitting it pays out a paper.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Light switch</td><td>Corridor, beside the women's door</td><td>A white rocker with a strip panel next to it. Flips the corridor lighting.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:01</span>
      <strong>The level card: "Level 25 / Restrooms"</strong>
      The recording opens on the card that names the room, and it is worth ten seconds of attention because it settles two things players argue about. It says <b>Level 25</b>, and it calls the room <b>Restrooms</b>, plural — the level is not a single washroom but the pair behind the corridor. It also confirms there is no difficulty badge and no reward preview other than the standard <b>+50</b> that every Chapter 2 room pays.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l25-level-card.jpg" alt="The Level 25 Restrooms title card in Rooms and Exits, over the dimmed corridor with three doors and a potted plant" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uopc_Ls173Q&t=1s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:01</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:03</span>
      <strong>The corridor, and the one thing you can already touch</strong>
      The card lifts and you get the only clean wide shot of the level. Left: a plain door with a small panel on it instead of a handle — that is a lock you will not open today. Middle: the <b>men's</b> door, male figure at eye height. Right: the <b>women's</b> door, female figure. Between the two labelled doors stands the <b>potted plant</b>, and the striped floor runs from the bottom of the frame to the exit doorway on the right.
      Nothing in this shot looks interactive, and the plant looks like decoration. It is not. It is the first move of the level, and the corridor is also where the level ends, so memorise the exit doorway now — the 4x4 panel you finish on sits right beside it.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uopc_Ls173Q&t=3s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:03</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 0:04</span>
      <strong>Tap the plant: the first Key is in the soil</strong>
      Tap the plant and the camera zooms into the pot against the brick wall. Tap it a second time and the plant gives the key up; by 0:12 the wide shot is back and the inventory has a new slot labelled <b>Key</b>. It is the plainest interaction in the level — no tool, no combine, no puzzle — which is exactly why it is the one people skip.
      This is the single most-missed interaction in the room, because it is a plant. Two taps, no puzzle, and it gates the entire men's side of the level.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l25-plant-key.jpg" alt="The potted plant in the corridor of Level 25, zoomed in against the brick wall, with the dark soil visible around its stems" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uopc_Ls173Q&t=4s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:04</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 0:14</span>
      <strong>Use the Key on the men's door</strong>
      Back in the corridor, select the <b>Key</b>, press <b>Use</b>, then tap the middle door. The door swings in and the camera follows it into the men's room. If you tap the door with the key still in the bag, or tap the women's door, nothing happens — and the women's door is not opened by this key at all, so do not stand there trying it.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uopc_Ls173Q&t=14s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:14</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 0:18</span>
      <strong>Gloves on the counter, and the shape of the room</strong>
      Inside, the first pick-up is a pair of <b>Gloves</b> lying on the green counter beside the sinks. Take them. They are the level's answer to "this fixture is filthy": the urinals and the drain both want a hand you are willing to put in them, and the gloves are what the game counts as that hand.
      While you are here, read the room properly. The green stalls are on the left with one door hanging open; the three urinals are straight ahead under the pipes; the sinks and the big mirror are on the right; the cleaning cart is parked in the middle of the floor; and the floor drain sits between the cart and the counter. That drain is where the level's only arithmetic lives.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l25-men-restroom.jpg" alt="The men's restroom in Level 25: green stalls on the left with one door open, three urinals under exposed pipes on the back wall, three basins under a wide gold-framed mirror, a cleaning cart mid-floor and the corridor door with a male figure on it" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uopc_Ls173Q&t=18s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:18</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 0:25</span>
