    <h2>The exit panel, cell by cell<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>This is the part of the level that no amount of exploring will solve on its own, so here is the whole grid. Rows run top to bottom as the panel is mounted; columns run left to right. A number is the count of clicks that cell wants; a greyed <b>0</b> is a cell no paper mentions and which you should leave alone.</p>

    <table class="matrix">
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>3</td><td class="zero">0</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>4</td><td class="zero">0</td><td class="zero">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td class="zero">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>4</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="matrix-cap">Clicks per cell, top row first — twenty-nine clicks in total.</p>

    <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l25-mosaic-complete.jpg" alt="The finished 4x4 mosaic panel in Level 25 with twelve patterned cells and four cells left plain grey" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />

    <p>Where each number comes from matters, because if your copy of the level differs the table is only as good as the papers behind it. Four pieces of paper are needed, each of which marks three cells and leaves the rest blank. Read as a set, the twelve marked cells account for every non-zero value above, and the four cells that no paper marks are the four zeros.</p>

    <table class="item-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Paper</th><th>Cells it marks</th><th>Values</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Paper A</td><td>Row 1 col 3 · Row 2 col 1 · Row 3 col 3</td><td>2 · 2 · 3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Paper B</td><td>Row 1 col 4 · Row 3 col 1 · Row 4 col 2</td><td>1 · 3 · 2</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Paper C</td><td>Row 2 col 2 · Row 4 col 1 · Row 4 col 3</td><td>4 · 2 · 2</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Paper D</td><td>Row 1 col 1 · Row 3 col 2 · Row 4 col 4</td><td>3 · 1 · 4</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The letters are ours, not the game's — the items are all just labelled <b>Piece of paper</b>, so the only way to tell them apart in the bag is to open each one and look at which cells are shaded. Paper B is the one we are most confident about, because it appears in both recordings and reads identically in each: a 1 in the top-right corner, a 3 in the third row on the left, and a 2 on the bottom row second from the left.</p>
    <p>Two practical notes. First, clicking a cell more than its number does not lock the panel — it cycles, so you can carry on clicking until the right pattern comes round. Second, the four unmarked cells are meant to stay plain: a finished panel is not a full mosaic, it is twelve patterned tiles and four grey ones, which is exactly what the recording shows at 9:10.</p>

    <h2>How the level is built: five locks in a row<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Strip away the taps and Level 25 is five gates in a line, and every one of them is fed by something from a different room. Seen in order, the dependencies are:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Plant → men's door.</b> Two taps on the corridor plant produce the first key. Without it the whole men's side of the level is sealed.</li>
      <li><b>Drain → soap.</b> The lid is arithmetic (four sides, 61 each, digit sums). The key inside it opens the soap dispenser, and the soap is what makes the towel able to clean a mirror.</li>
      <li><b>Lamp → mirror → paper.</b> The nail file opens the caged lamp, the bulb from that lamp goes into the empty holder over the right-hand mirror, and the lit mirror pays out a paper. A tool and its reward sit in different rooms, which is the one genuinely confusing hop in the level.</li>
      <li><b>Button → dryer → paper.</b> The machine button fits the hand dryer's empty socket and the dryer dispenses another paper. Nothing signposts the dryer as broken.</li>
      <li><b>Papers → panel.</b> Four grids, twelve marked cells, twenty-nine clicks. Solving it ends the level outright — there is no sixth gate.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Everything else in the two rooms is either a tool for one of those gates or scenery. The gloves, the plunger, the spray bottle, the towel, the hook, the nail file, the utensil and the machine button all feed one of the five; the urinals, the toilet paper, the mop and the framed mirrors do not feed anything we could see.</p>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The plant.</b> It is the level's first move and it looks like decoration. If the men's door will not open and you have not tapped the pot twice, that is why.</li>
      <li><b>Adding the tiles as two-digit numbers.</b> The drain says 61 for a reason: every side has to reach sixty-one by adding the individual digits. Add 78 as seventy-eight and every side overshoots by design.</li>
      <li><b>Looking for a code.</b> The <b>23919</b> on the PIPES poster is the loudest number in the level and it opens nothing. So is the moment you realise there is no keypad anywhere — this room does not have one.</li>
      <li><b>The missing paper.</b> Three papers are obvious pick-ups; the fourth is behind a poster, inside a wall compartment, rolled up. Players who have three grids and cannot make the panel work are usually short the one from the hand dryer instead.</li>
      <li><b>Trying to clean the mirror with a dry towel.</b> The towel has to go through the soap and the spray bottle first. A dry towel on a dirty mirror does nothing and gives no feedback at all.</li>
      <li><b>Waiting for a door at the end.</b> The panel is the last interaction. There is no exit key, no door icon and no final tap — the results screen arrives by itself.</li>
      <li><b>Walking the women's room before the lights.</b> In the dark most of its pick-ups do not sparkle, and the pouch in particular is easy to miss when the room is grey.</li>
    </ul>

