    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <div class="warn">
      <b>Eight things, all of them stated plainly rather than guessed at:</b>
      <ul>
        <li><b>The drain's ring layout.</b> The centre <b>61</b> and the rule — each side totals 61, digits added separately, a 78 counting as 7+8 — were read twice and are solid. What we did not reconstruct is a ring-by-ring solution map: the tiles rotate faster than one frame per second resolves, so printing "put this tile here" would mean inventing the layout. The rule is enough to solve it; a position map from us would not be.</li>
        <li><b>Whether the four unnumbered cells are exactly the four that stay plain.</b> The papers mark twelve cells and the finished panel shows twelve patterned tiles with four plain, which lines up. But at the resolution of the recordings we could not map each plain tile to a specific row and column with confidence, so the instruction to leave the unnumbered cells alone is an inference from the papers rather than a frame-by-frame read of the solved board.</li>
        <li><b>Which tool clears the sink.</b> Both recordings show an item leaving the bag while the basin is zoomed and the water draining, but not which one — the footage is ambiguous between the plunger and the pipe at that moment. What is not ambiguous is the payout: a <b>Crumpled piece of paper</b>, which dismantles into a paper and a key.</li>
        <li><b>What the light switch's strip panel is for.</b> The rocker is unambiguous and the corridor lights respond to it. The grey strips and the red and green lamps beside it are never touched in either recording, and we never see a wrong state rejected, so this page does not pretend to know what they check.</li>
        <li><b>The wall compartment behind the PIPES poster.</b> We can see the torn poster, the recessed panel and the rolled paper coming out. We never see the panel being unlocked, so we cannot say whether it needs a key or simply opens once the poster is broken — the level's keys and locks do not map one-to-one anywhere in this room.</li>
        <li><b>Which door the 4x4 panel belongs to.</b> It sits beside the exit doorway in the corridor, and solving it ends the level directly — no door swings, no exit icon appears, the camera simply cuts. Anything beyond "the panel is the exit" would be our reading of the set dressing rather than something the footage shows.</li>
        <li><b>The efficiency meter's value.</b> The results screen has a segmented efficiency bar and we could not read it consistently: three separate passes over the same frame counted seven, eight and ten filled segments. The bar exists, it is not printed as a number anywhere on screen, and we would rather say that than pick the reading we liked best. The reward itself — <b>+50</b>, <b>Time: 9:01</b>, bank at <b>200</b> — was confirmed three times.</li>
        <li><b>Two item pick-ups.</b> The wrench and the machine button both appear in the inventory between frames, with the camera on something else at the time. Their uses are visible — the wrench on the pipework, the button in the hand dryer's socket — but the exact fixture each one is taken from is not.</li>
      </ul>
      Everything else on this page — the three doors and their symbols, the plant's two pay-outs, the cart's three tools, the drain opening, the four paper grids, the panel's instruction text, the <b>+50</b> and the <b>9:01</b> — was read at least twice, from two recordings, and agrees.
    </div>

    <p>The honest summary is that Level 25 is a good room with two real puzzles and a lot of small errands, and the errands are what make it feel unfair. The drain and the paper grid are both fair; the plant, the lamp, the dryer and the poster are all hidden in plain sight, and none of them announces itself. If you take one thing from this page, take the order: plant, drain, soap, lamp, dryer, papers, panel.</p>

